diagnosis_text:
I cannot reproduce any outage of the web servers or the server-access switch. From both client networks (host_1_1_1_1 and host_2_1_1_1), HTTP to all four servers is fast and successful by IP and by DNS name. Evidence:
- HTTP probes: curl from both hosts to http://10.200.0.3–.6 and http://web0.local–web3.local completed in ~2–5 ms total with consistent TTFB, indicating successful connections and responses.
- DNS: host resolv.conf points at 10.200.0.2; the DNS server is authoritative for “local” and serves A records for web0–web3 (db.local). getent on both hosts resolves web0.local–web3.local correctly.
- Services: netstat on all web servers shows 0.0.0.0:80 LISTEN. Pings across the topology (including to 10.200.0.4) succeed with low RTT.
This mechanism shows Layer 3/4 reachability and Layer 7 HTTP service all healthy, localizing no fault on switch_server_access or the servers themselves.

Root cause: No network-side or server-side fault could be found; the issue appears transient or client-specific (e.g., wrong URL/hostname, cached DNS, or browser state) rather than an infrastructure failure.

Corrective fix: No changes required in the network. Ask the user to provide the exact hostnames/URLs that “vanished,” clear browser/DNS cache, and retry. If the sites are expected to be HTTPS-only, confirm the intended protocol/hostnames; current servers expose HTTP on port 80 and respond normally. === END ===